\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Shared notation.}$\mathbb N$, $\mathbb Z$, $\mathbb Q$, $\mathbb R$, and $\mathbb C$ denote the natural numbers, integers, rationals, reals, and complex numbers, respectively. All additional parameters and hypotheses are specified in the question.


\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
Let $M$ and $N$ be closed aspherical manifolds, meaning that their universal covers are contractible. If $\pi_1(M)$ and $\pi_1(N)$ are isomorphic, must $M$ and $N$ be homeomorphic? This is the topological rigidity question; no diffeomorphism is demanded.
\par\smallskip\textit{Formulation references:} \href{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}{[S1]}, \href{https://arxiv.org/abs/0902.2480}{[S2]}.

\subsection{Short English statement}
Let $M$ and $N$ be closed aspherical manifolds, meaning that their universal covers are contractible. If $\pi_1(M)$ and $\pi_1(N)$ are isomorphic, must $M$ and $N$ be homeomorphic? This is the topological rigidity question; no diffeomorphism is demanded.

